{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgwKaxpctabMTXfVPaUja","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01Hky5zrWsXkvE8gYVt4yBvy","name":"Bash","input":{"command":"cd /workspace/paper; grep -n 'section\\|cite' paper.tex | head -150"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":4383,"cache_read_input_tokens":16566,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":4383},"output_tokens":32,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f21b9f4-b86d-4d3e-8ede-6d48e81a6222","uuid":"0995e4e9-27b5-4bf7-bb27-2fab5a0d7374","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:45:42.715Z","request_id":"req_011CgwKaxdDShtqVbKVUfhBM"}
{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01Hky5zrWsXkvE8gYVt4yBvy","type":"tool_result","content":"58:\\section{Introduction}\n59:Prompt and pipeline optimizers such as DSPy and MIPRO search over instructions, demonstrations and multi-call structures on a benchmark sample \\citep{Khattab2023DSPy,OpsahlOng2024MIPRO}, and in practice the best configuration is often reported with the score it attained on the very sample used to select it. Configuration search is also cast as best-arm identification under a fixed evaluation budget \\citep{Shi2024PromptBAI}. The reported number is the maximum, over many candidates, of noisy estimates, and the candidate that attains it was chosen \\emph{because} its estimate was high. It is therefore a biased estimate of that candidate's accuracy on new questions, and the usual interval around it ignores the selection. Whether comparisons and decisions built on such numbers are trustworthy depends on how large the gap is and on what controls it.\n63:Our answer to Q2 is a post-hoc \\emph{reporter} that never touches the search: it receives only the evaluation log of (question, configuration, outcome) triples, the selected configuration and basic metadata, and returns a point estimate and a 90\\% interval for the selected configuration's fresh-question accuracy. The reporter fits a hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model with empirical-Bayes variance components (EB-IRT). Because both searchers we study choose using only observed outcomes, selection is ignorable for the likelihood, so one fitting routine serves exhaustive search and successive halving. Shrinkage of configuration effects addresses the winner's curse directly; the interval is formed from posterior quantiles, which are the optimal report under the interval score \\citep{Gneiting2007Scoring}.\n65:We test reliability with an evaluator that owns the data-generating worlds, the searchers and the truth, so that coverage and interval score are measured against known fresh-question accuracy. Worlds are synthetic latent-difficulty worlds and cached real outcomes of Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct \\citep{Qwen2024Qwen25} on MMLU-Pro \\citep{Wang2024MMLUPro} under 24 prompt configurations, plus three negative controls.\n78:\\section{Related Work}\n79:\\textbf{Adaptive data analysis.} Reusing a holdout set across adaptively chosen analyses invalidates standard inference. \\citet{Dwork2014PreservingSV,Dwork2015GeneralizationIA} showed that differential-privacy-style noise preserves validity, \\citet{Dwork2015TheRH} gave the reusable holdout, \\citet{Feldman2017CalibratingNT} calibrated the noise to variance, and \\citet{Marchant2024AdaptiveDA} extended the setting to growing data; \\citet{Hardt2014Hard} showed that preventing false discovery in fully interactive analysis is computationally hard, and \\citet{Ullman2017TechnicalPB,Dwork2017GuiltfreeDR} give overviews. The Ladder \\citep{Blum2015Ladder} reports leaderboard scores of repeated submissions through a thresholded mechanism. These methods change \\emph{what is released} to the analyst. Our reporter instead leaves the search untouched and corrects the report afterwards from the log; it does not provide their worst-case guarantees, and we do not study adaptive reporting across several released scores.\n81:\\textbf{Inference after selection.} Post-selection inference constructs valid intervals for selected targets \\citep{Berk2013ValidPSI}; \\citet{Andrews2024InferenceOnWinners} study inference on the best-performing unit, the direct statistical analogue of a best-of-search score; and \\citet{Chernozhukov2013Gaussian} give Gaussian approximations for maxima that underlie bootstrap critical values. Empirical-Bayes shrinkage \\citep{Efron1973Stein} and its connection to selection bias \\citep{Efron2011Tweedie} motivate our use of a hierarchical model. The bootstrap optimism correction \\citep{Efron1997Bootstrap632} motivates the question-resampling baseline. Benchmark overfitting under repeated reuse has been probed on image classification by collecting a fresh test set \\citep{Recht2019ImageNet}.\n83:\\textbf{Prompt search and LLM evaluation.} DSPy \\citep{Khattab2023DSPy} and MIPRO \\citep{OpsahlOng2024MIPRO} automate prompt and pipeline optimization, best-arm formulations bound evaluation cost \\citep{Shi2024PromptBAI}, successive halving \\citep{Jamieson2016SuccessiveHalving} is a standard budget allocator, performance is sensitive to prompt formatting \\citep{Sclar2023Quantifying}, multi-prompt evaluation can be made sample-efficient \\citep{Polo2024EfficientMultiPrompt}, and error bars on benchmark scores have been advocated \\citep{Miller2024ErrorBars}. None of these works supplies a post-search score with selection-aware uncertainty. We use the Wilson interval \\citep{Wilson1927Probable} for the baselines.\n85:\\section{Method}\\label{sec:method}\n86:\\subsection{Problem setup and success criterion}\n91:which is a proper scoring rule minimized in expectation by the $\\alpha/2$ and $1-\\alpha/2$ predictive quantiles \\citep{Gneiting2007Scoring}. A missed replication costs $20$ per unit of miss distance, so over-narrow intervals are penalized heavily. We also report coverage, mean width, signed bias of $\\hat\\theta$ and RMSE. A reporter that fails on a replication receives the worst-case score $20$.\n93:\\subsection{Design rationale}\n96:\\subsection{EB-IRT reporter}\n117:\\subsection{Compared reporters}\\label{sec:baselines}\n120:\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\\label{sec:protocol}\n131:\\section{Results}\\label{sec:results}\n132:\\subsection{Optimism of best-of-search reporting}\\label{sec:gap}\n146:\\subsection{Main comparison: sealed final evaluation}\n213:\\subsection{Where the gain is large, and where it is not}\n241:\\subsection{Negative controls}\\label{sec:controls}\n249:\\subsection{Reporter variants considered during development}\n275:\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n285:\\section{Conclusion}\n299:\\section{Supplementary Information}\n300:\\subsection{Estimator details and hyperparameters}\n326:\\subsection{Evaluation protocol and provenance}\n334:\\subsection{Per-case detail}\n385:\\subsection{Reproducibility}\n388:\\subsection{LLM Usage}","is_error":false}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"1f21b9f4-b86d-4d3e-8ede-6d48e81a6222","uuid":"ca8f6905-1a17-4eed-b5de-e880ebb7e7e7","timestamp":"2026-11-11T14:45:42.777Z","tool_use_result":{"stdout":"58:\\section{Introduction}\n59:Prompt and pipeline optimizers such as DSPy and MIPRO search over instructions, demonstrations and multi-call structures on a benchmark sample \\citep{Khattab2023DSPy,OpsahlOng2024MIPRO}, and in practice the best configuration is often reported with the score it attained on the very sample used to select it. Configuration search is also cast as best-arm identification under a fixed evaluation budget \\citep{Shi2024PromptBAI}. The reported number is the maximum, over many candidates, of noisy estimates, and the candidate that attains it was chosen \\emph{because} its estimate was high. It is therefore a biased estimate of that candidate's accuracy on new questions, and the usual interval around it ignores the selection. Whether comparisons and decisions built on such numbers are trustworthy depends on how large the gap is and on what controls it.\n63:Our answer to Q2 is a post-hoc \\emph{reporter} that never touches the search: it receives only the evaluation log of (question, configuration, outcome) triples, the selected configuration and basic metadata, and returns a point estimate and a 90\\% interval for the selected configuration's fresh-question accuracy. The reporter fits a hierarchical logistic latent-difficulty model with empirical-Bayes variance components (EB-IRT). Because both searchers we study choose using only observed outcomes, selection is ignorable for the likelihood, so one fitting routine serves exhaustive search and successive halving. Shrinkage of configuration effects addresses the winner's curse directly; the interval is formed from posterior quantiles, which are the optimal report under the interval score \\citep{Gneiting2007Scoring}.\n65:We test reliability with an evaluator that owns the data-generating worlds, the searchers and the truth, so that coverage and interval score are measured against known fresh-question accuracy. Worlds are synthetic latent-difficulty worlds and cached real outcomes of Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct \\citep{Qwen2024Qwen25} on MMLU-Pro \\citep{Wang2024MMLUPro} under 24 prompt configurations, plus three negative controls.\n78:\\section{Related Work}\n79:\\textbf{Adaptive data analysis.} Reusing a holdout set across adaptively chosen analyses invalidates standard inference. \\citet{Dwork2014PreservingSV,Dwork2015GeneralizationIA} showed that differential-privacy-style noise preserves validity, \\citet{Dwork2015TheRH} gave the reusable holdout, \\citet{Feldman2017CalibratingNT} calibrated the noise to variance, and \\citet{Marchant2024AdaptiveDA} extended the setting to growing data; \\citet{Hardt2014Hard} showed that preventing false discovery in fully interactive analysis is computationally hard, and \\citet{Ullman2017TechnicalPB,Dwork2017GuiltfreeDR} give overviews. The Ladder \\citep{Blum2015Ladder} reports leaderboard scores of repeated submissions through a thresholded mechanism. These methods change \\emph{what is released} to the analyst. Our reporter instead leaves the search untouched and corrects the report afterwards from the log; it does not provide their worst-case guarantees, and we do not study adaptive reporting across several released scores.\n81:\\textbf{Inference after selection.} Post-selection inference constructs valid intervals for selected targets \\citep{Berk2013ValidPSI}; \\citet{Andrews2024InferenceOnWinners} study inference on the best-performing unit, the direct statistical analogue of a best-of-search score; and \\citet{Chernozhukov2013Gaussian} give Gaussian