n{EB-IRT reporter}\n% @evidence[[analysis/kappa_values.json]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/reporter_eb_irt/reporter.py]]\n\\textbf{Model.} With $\\sigma$ the logistic function, each logged outcome follows\n\\begin{equation}\ny_{qk}\\sim\\mathrm{Bernoulli}\\big(\\sigma(a_k-b_q)\\big),\\quad a_k\\sim\\mathcal N(0,\\tau^2),\\quad b_q\\sim\\mathcal N(m_{s(q)},s^2),\n\\end{equation}\nwhere $a_k$ is a configuration effect, $b_q$ a question difficulty and $m_{s}$ the mean difficulty of subject $s$ (the question's subject code; synthetic worlds have a single code). Subject means are shrunk toward the global mean with five pseudo-questions. The fresh-question accuracy of configuration $k$ is $\\theta_k=\\mathbb E_b[\\sigma(a_k-b)]$, where $b$ is drawn from the mixture of subjects weighted by the observed subject frequencies.\n\n\\textbf{Empirical-Bayes fit.} Using only the sparse triples, we alternate damped Newton sweeps for $(a,b)$ with variance updates $\\tau^2\\leftarrow\\frac1K\\big(\\sum_k\\hat a_k^2+\\sum_k S_k^{-1}\\big)$ and $s^2\\leftarrow\\frac1N\\big(\\sum_q(\\hat b_q-m_{s(q)})^2+\\sum_q H_q^{-1}\\big)$, where $S_k$ is the Schur-complement posterior precision of $a_k$ after integrating out difficulties and $H_q$ the diagonal Hessian for $b_q$. These Laplace corrections stop $\\tau^2$ from collapsing or inflating. Fitting uses bincount-based vector operations, so exhaustive and halving logs share one code path; the fit stops after at most 30 sweeps or a 1.5\\,s wall-clock budget.\n\n\\textbf{Ignorability.} Exhaustive search and successive halving choose which entries to observe and which configuration to select using only previously observed outcomes. The likelihood of the log is therefore unaffected by the design, and a likelihood-based posterior is valid under either searcher without a searcher-specific correction, provided the model is adequately specified. This is a motivation, not a proof for the real-outcome worlds, whose subject-by-configuration structure 