100 random half-splits; on each, the best configuration on one half is re-scored on the other, the mean score gap $g$ is rescaled to $\\hat p_{\\hat k}-g/\\sqrt2$, and a Wilson interval is placed around it.\n\n\\subsection{Experimental protocol}\\label{sec:protocol}\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/evaluate_final.py#CASES]]\n% @evidence[[source_evidence/evaluator/final_manifest.json]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json#max_abs_diff_vs_recorded]]\n% @evidence[[paper_data.json#final.metric.completed_replication_fraction]]\n\\textbf{Worlds.} \\emph{Synthetic} worlds draw a base accuracy uniformly from $[0.25,0.75]$ and per-configuration accuracies with spread $0.03$; outcomes follow a Gaussian copula with latent correlation $0.5$ across configurations, and the truth is the selected configuration's accuracy. \\emph{Real} worlds use a cached $1500\\times24$ binary tensor of Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct answers on MMLU-Pro (24 prompt configurations: four instruction wordings $\\times$ three demonstration settings $\\times$ two answer-prefix formats; revisions in the Supplement). Each replication samples $N$ questions without replacement, permutes configuration labels, searches, and takes as truth the selected configuration's accuracy on the remaining pool questions. \\emph{Controls}: N1 is a synthetic world with identical configurations; N2 is the real pool with each question's configuration columns reassigned by a random derangement to break configuration effects; Z1 is an all-zero world with $K=8$. In any null world no configuration is better, so a reporter should neither invent signal nor lose coverage.\n\n\\textbf{Cases and searchers.} Table~\\ref{tab:cases} lists the cases. \\emph{Exhaustive} search evaluates every configuration on every question and selects the highest total; \\emph{successive halving} works with a nominal budget of $N\\!\\cdot\\!K$ evaluations over $\\lceil\\log_2K\\rceil$ rounds, discarding the lower half of the survivors each round, so configurations eliminated early are observed on few questions and the log is sparse. Each case has $R=30$ replications ($R=10$ for Z1).\n\n\\textbf{Aggregation and uncertainty.} Per-case values average replications; the primary aggregate averages P1--P2 (synthetic, weight 0.4) and P3--P6 (real, weight 0.6) within family. The sealed evaluation uses eight seeds (9101--9108), each running all nine cases. We report mean $\\pm$ standard deviation of seed-level values and, for reporter differences, paired seed-level differences. The evaluator records interval score, bias, coverage and width for the EB-IRT reporter and interval score and bias for the baselines; for baseline coverage and width we replayed the evaluator's own deterministic world, search and baseline code, which reproduced all recorded baseline interval scores and biases exactly (maximum absolute difference 0), and report the derived coverage and width. Per-case records carry an unverified diagnostics label from the evaluator, so we treat aggregate metrics as authoritative and per-case values as supporting. The reporter was frozen before the sealed evaluation, and the evaluation completed all replications (completed fraction 1.0).\n\n\\section{Discussion and Limitations}\\label{sec:limits}\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#aggregate_primary]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/final_per_case.json#paired_diff_aggregate]]\n% @evidence[[analysis/reconstructed_baselines.json#aggregate_primary]]\n\\textbf{What the evidence supports.} In the studied worlds, best-of-search reporting is optimistic by $0.037$ absolute on average and its nominal interval is badly calibrated, a gap that depends on the question budget and on how distinguishable the configurations are. A hierarchical posterior interval fitted to the log lowers the interval score by about a third relative to the naive report and by a quarter relative to Bonferroni-Wilson with lower width. These statements hold for eight seeds of a sealed run that completed with a qualified outcome.\n\n\\textbf{How to test a reporter (Q2).} Our results suggest what a reliability test should contain. (a) Worlds with known truth, so that coverage and bias are measured rather than inferred. (b) Interval score together with coverage, since either alone is gamed by extreme widths: Bonferroni-Wilson reaches coverage $0.96$ only by being $47\\%$ wider than EB-IRT. (c) Results by regime (search procedure, question budget, world), because the aggregate coverage of $0.929$ conceals cases from $0.83$ to $0.98$. (d) Negative controls: a null world to catch invented signal, a constant world to catch degenerate behaviour. (e) Seed-level uncertainty and paired differences between reporters, since the naive and resampling scores vary across seeds with standard deviations of $0.023$ and $0.025$, against $0.004$ for EB-IRT. (f) Baselines matched to the searcher: against resampling alone, the exhaustive-search verdict (a tie) differs from the halving verdict (a large gain).\n\n\\textbf{What it does not support.} (1) \\emph{Generality}: one 0.5B model whose accuracies (0.11--0.17) are close to the 10-option chance level of 0.10, one benchmark, one prompt family; the configuration spread and the optimism may differ for stronger models. (2) \\emph{Searchers}: only evaluator-owned exhaustive and halving searchers; no DSPy or MIPRO run, and no adaptive reporting of several scores in the manner of the Ladder. (3) \\emph{Decisions}: we measured the gap in a single configuration's accuracy, not whether it reverses rankings or deployment decisions; coverage and miss penalties show that reported numbers are unreliable, not how often a conclusion changes. (4) \\emph{Attribution}: the $\\kappa$ widening is hand-set and was never switched off, and subject means, EB variances and the Laplace correction were not ablated, so we cannot say how much coverage comes from the model versus the widening. The bias comparison is unaffected by $\\kappa$. (5) \\emph{Misspecification}: one-dimensional difficulty is a poor fit for real subject-by-configuration structure; the extension that added such structure did not help in development. (6) \\emph{Residual failures}: undercoverage at $N=600$ and in halving (0.83--0.89), positive bias in null worlds, and no gain over naive in P4 and P6. (7) \\emph{Theory}: ignorability is an argument, not a guarantee for misspecified models, and we offer no coverage theorem. (8) \\emph{Provenance}: the real outcome tensors were produced by the task authors and not regenerated, per-case diagnostics are labelled unverified, and the evaluator's gate diagnostics beyond the outcome are unavailable, so we make no claim about why individual checks passed. The qualified outcome does not turn these limits into guarantees.","stderr":"","interrupted":false,"isImage":false,"noOutputExpected":false}}
